  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday) \nIssued at 1021 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nChilly weather sticks with us on Monday with surface flow remaining \neasterly throughout the day as a surface ridge axis positions from \nthe upper midwest all the way southward into TX. Despite mainly \nclear skies, high temperatures on Monday are likely to remain in the \nmid to upper 40s area-wide. The forecast becomes a bit more \ninteresting by Monday night, with most models depicting a relatively \npotent mid/upper level trough axis digging southward over the Four \nCorners overnight and reaching West TX by Tuesday morning. Large-\nscale forcing for ascent appears fairly strong with this system, and \nsome banded precipitation could develop as a zone of modest midlevel \nFn sets up over the region. With a cold airmass already in place, \nthis precipitation will likely be in the form of light snow showers \nor a rain/snow mix, but there is plenty of uncertainty regarding how \nheavy and widespread this precip will be. Some light snow \naccumulations will be possible Tuesday morning mainly over \nnorthwestern portions of the Caprock, but it is too early to \ndetermine anything more specific regarding timing or precise \naccumulations. Precip will then end fairly quickly on Tuesday \nafternoon as the system departs, but it will still be a very cold \nday by November standards with highs Tuesday likely to only reach \nthe upper 30s to low 40s. \n\nModel consensus then suggests that the rest of the week will be \nmainly dry, with guidance continuing to back off on any potential \nfor widespread precipitation over Thanksgiving and into the holiday \nweekend. Still, temperatures will struggle to warm much at all \ncompared to earlier in the week as cool surface ridging holds firm \nover most of the central CONUS. High temperatures are currently \nexpected to remain in the 40s to low 50s each day from Wednesday \nthrough at least Friday.",
